    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_288ef0158e3adbd5f00d8c4f329f66cd\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"modifications identified via mouse selections that potently targets brain endothelial cells in rodents and CNS neurons in macaques after intravenous injection^20.\\nWe next assessed the validated AAV interactors for their potential to explain the enhanced brain tropisms of the engineered capsids. Sorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB^47 spotlighted a specific interaction of LRP6 with AAV9-X1.1 (Fig. 4a).\\nThis capsid displays enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque (Table 1)^20.\\nWe leveraged this property to determine the in vivo effects of AAV9-X1.1’s LRP6 interaction (Fig. 5a). Brain endothelium-targeted AAV1-X1 packaging either control mCherry or Cre recombinase was systemically administered to Lrp6 Cre-conditional knockout mice.\\nAfter three weeks, either AAV9-based PHP.eB or AAV9-X1.1 packaging eGFP was systemically delivered. Whereas PHP.eB showed characteristic strong brain transduction regardless of AAV1-X1 cargo, AAV9-X1.1 brain endothelial tropism was markedly reduced in the AAV1-X1-dosed mice with Lrp6 knocked out in AAV1-X1 transfected cells (Fig.\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the previously-characterized enhanced in vivo brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates^20, we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Fig. 6a, b).\\nHuman brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus macaque primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the vendors’ instructions.\\nBiomaterials288, 121701 (2022). DOI: 10.1016/j.biomaterials.2022.121701 PMID: 35985893 PMCID: PMC9621732\\n39. Jang, S., Shen, H. K., Ding, X., Miles, T. F. & Gradinaru, V. Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB. Mol. Ther. - Methods Clin. Dev.26, 343–354 (2022).\\nDOI: 10.1074/jbc.M807285200 PMID: 19339249 PMCID: PMC2685710\\n54. Krolak, T. et al. A high-efficiency AAV for endothelial cell transduction throughout the central nervous system. Nat. Cardiovasc Res1, 389–400 (2022). DOI: 10.1038/s44161-022-00046-4 PMID: 35571675 PMCID: PMC9103166\\n55. Lin, C. et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39245720\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder - PubMed\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-08-09\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\\nAffiliations Expand\\nAffiliations\\nContributed equally.\\na Left: Arraying AAV capsid-specific hits…\\nFig. 4. Primate brain-enhanced AAVs gain interaction with LRP6.\\na Left: Arraying AAV capsid-specific hits by human brain endothelial cell expression levels reveals highly-conserved LRP6 as a potential receptor for BBB crossing.\\nContributed equally.\\nLRP6 modulates CNS function of…\\nFig. 5. LRP6 modulates CNS function of engineered AAVs in mice.\\na Schematic of Lrp6 …\\nFig. 5. LRP6 modulates CNS function of engineered AAVs in mice.\\na Schematic of Lrp6 conditional knockout by sequential AAV injection.\\nContributed equally.\\nCre-conditional Lrp6 knockout mice were systemically injected with AAV1-X1 packaging either Cre or mCherry, creating cohorts of mice that differ in their Lrp6 expression. After allowing time for expression, these cohorts were each injected with AAV9-PHP.eB or AAV9-X1.1 packaging eGFP.\\nContributed equally.\\nc Quantification of AAV potency demonstrating that conditional knockout of Lrp6 in mouse selectively and potently reduces AAV9-X1.1 brain and liver gene delivery.\\nContributed equally.\\nLRP6 enhances engineered AAV potency in primate BBB and neuronal cell culture.\\nAAV9-X1.1 has enhanced potency in ( a ) macaque and ( b ) human primary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture (black data points), which decreases to AAV9 levels with Mesd inhibition of LRP6 (blue data points).\\nContributed equally.\\n2025 Aug 6;33(8):3902-3916. doi: 10.1016/j.ymthe.2025.04.046. Epub 2025 May 8. Mol Ther. 2025. PMID: 40340250\\n* Targeting AAV vectors to the central nervous system by engineering capsid-receptor interactions that enable crossing of the blood-brain barrier.\\nContributed equally.\\nPMID: 40868246 Free PMC article. Review.\\n* Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids.\\nBrittain TJ, Jang S, Coughlin GM, Barcelona BH, Giriat I, Ristic F, Appling N, Chossis CP, Shay TF, Gradinaru V. Brittain TJ, et al. bioRxiv [Preprint]. 2025 Jun 3:2025.06.02.655683.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0?code=e0a1e855-a690-4f4b-8313-dbb4703d692d&error=cookies_not_supported\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder | Nature Communications\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We next assessed the validated AAV interactors for their potential to explain the enhanced brain tropisms of the engineered capsids. Sorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB 47 spotlighted a specific interaction of LRP6 with AAV9-X1.1 (Fig. 4a ).\\nThis capsid displays enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque (Table 1 ) 20 .\\n4: Primate brain-enhanced AAVs gain interaction with LRP6.\\nFull size image\\na Left: Arraying AAV capsid-specific hits by human brain endothelial cell expression levels reveals highly-conserved LRP6 as a potential receptor for BBB crossing. Right: the LRP6 extracellular domain contains 4 YWTD domains (E1-E4).\\n```\\nimilar to that of AAV9 (Supplementary Fig. [9b](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#MOESM1) ). In contrast, AAV9-X1.1, like PHP.eB <sup>[39](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR39 \\\"Jang, S., Shen, H. K., Ding, X., Miles, T. F. & Gradinaru, V. Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB. Mol. Ther. - Methods Clin. Dev. 26,\\n```\\nAAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac potently infect HEK293 cells, with AAV9-X1.1 having a stronger effect (Supplementary Fig. 10a ). To determine if this potency is mediated by endogenous LRP6 expression in HEK293 cells, we tested the effect of LRP6 inhibitors.\\nWhile AAV9-X1.1 productively engages LRP6 at the lower endogenous expression levels of LRP6 on HEK293 cells, CAP-Mac shows greater potency when LRP6 is overexpressed.\\nWe leveraged this property to determine the in vivo effects of AAV9-X1.1’s LRP6 interaction (Fig. 5a ). Brain endothelium-targeted AAV1-X1 packaging either control mCherry or Cre recombinase was systemically administered to Lrp6 Cre-conditional knockout mice.\\nAfter three weeks, either AAV9-based PHP.eB or AAV9-X1.1 packaging eGFP was systemically delivered. Whereas PHP.eB showed characteristic strong brain transduction regardless of AAV1-X1 cargo, AAV9-X1.1 brain endothelial tropism was markedly reduced in the AAV1-X1-dosed mice with Lrp6 knocked out in AAV1-X1 transfected cells (Fig.\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the previously-characterized enhanced in vivo brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates 20 , we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Fig. 6 a, b ).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1.full-text\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain-barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder | bioRxiv\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-01-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"LRP6 modulates CNS function of engineered AAVs in mice.\\na , Schematic of Lrp6 conditional knockout by sequential AAV injection. Lrp6 Cre-conditional knockout mice are systemically injected with AAV1-X1 packaging either Cre or mCherry, creating cohorts of mice that differ in their LRP6 expression.\\nImaging parameters were optimized independently for AAV9-X1.1 and AAV9-PHP.eB second dose conditions. c , Quantification of AAV potency demonstrating that conditional knockout of Lrp6 in mouse brain selectively and potently reduces AAV9-X1.1 brain and liver potency.\\nd-e , AAV9-X1.1 has enhanced potency in d , macaque and e, human primary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture, which decreases to AAV9 levels with Mesd inhibition of LRP6. Bars indicate the mean value.\\nSorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB 45 spotlighted a specific interaction of low-density-lipoprotein-receptor-related-protein 6 (LRP6) with AAV9-X1.1 ( Figure 3a ).\\nThis capsid displays an enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to an enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque ( Table 1 ) 20 .\\nWhereas PHP.eB showed characteristic strong brain transduction regardless of AAV1-X1 cargo, AAV9-X1.1 brain endothelial tropism was markedly reduced in the AAV1-X1-dosed mice with Lrp6 knocked out in AAV1-X1 transfected cells ( Figure 4b and c ), confirming LRP6’s necessity for capsid BBB entry in vivo .\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates, we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture ( Figure 4d and 4e ). AAV9-X1.1 was markedly more potent than its parent, AAV9, in the PBMECs from both species.\\nHuman brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus monkey primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the instructions provided by the vendor.\\nMultimodal imaging of capsid and cargo reveals differential brain targeting and liver detargeting of systemically-administered AAVs . Biomaterials 288 , 121701 ( 2022 ).\\n39. 39. ↵\\nJang , S. , Shen , H. K. , Ding , X. , Miles , T. F. & Gradinaru , V . Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP . eB.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain-barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-01-05\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Sorting the screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB^45 spotlighted a specific interaction of low-density-lipoprotein-receptor-related-protein 6 (LRP6) with AAV9-X1.1 (Figure 3a).\\nThis capsid displays an enhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to an enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque (Table 1)^20.\\nWe leveraged this property to determine the in vivo effects of AAV9-X1.1’s LRP6 interaction (Figure 4a). Brain endothelium-targeted AAV1-X1 packaging either control mCherry or Cre recombinase was systemically administered to Lrp6 Cre-conditional knockout mice.\\nWhereas PHP.eB showed characteristic strong brain transduction regardless of AAV1-X1 cargo, AAV9-X1.1 brain endothelial tropism was markedly reduced in the AAV1-X1-dosed mice with Lrp6 knocked out in AAV1-X1 transfected cells (Figure 4b and c), confirming LRP6’s necessity for capsid BBB entry in vivo.\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates, we tested the vector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Figure 4d and 4e). AAV9-X1.1 was markedly more potent than its parent, AAV9, in the PBMECs from both species.\\nHuman brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus monkey primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the instructions provided by the vendor.\\nper animal of AAV1-X1 packaging either Ef1a-mCherry or Ef1A-Cre (N=6 per condition). After 3 weeks, those mice were re-administered with 1 x 10^12 v.g. per animal of PHP.eB or AAV-X1.1 packaging CAG-eGFP (N=3 per condition). Mice were randomly assigned to a particular AAV condition.\\nMultimodal imaging of capsid and cargo reveals differential brain targeting and liver detargeting of systemically-administered AAVs. Biomaterials 288, 121701 (2022).\\n39. Jang, S., Shen, H. K., Ding, X., Miles, T. F. & Gradinaru, V. Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB.\\nA High-Efficiency AAV for Endothelial Cell Transduction Throughout the Central Nervous System. Nat Cardiovasc Res 1, 389–400 (2022).\\n53. Lin, C. et al. Mesd is a general inhibitor of different Wnt ligands in Wnt/LRP signaling and inhibits PC-3 tumor growth in vivo. FEBS Letters 585, 3120–3125 (2011).\\n54. Kim, J. et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.01.05.574399v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Human cell surface-AAV interactomes identify LRP6 as blood-brain ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"screen hits by their expression level in endothelial cells of the human BBB 45 spotlighted a specific interaction of\\nlow-density-lipoprotein-receptor-related-protein 6 (LRP6) with AAV9-X1.1 (Figure 3a). This capsid displays an\\nenhanced brain endothelial-specific tropism in mice that shifts to an enhanced neuronal tropism in macaque\\n(Table 1) 20 .\\nsystemic dosing in mice previously exposed to AAV9-based vectors 20 . We leveraged this property to determine\\nthe in vivo effects of AAV9-X1.1’s LRP6 interaction (Figure 4a). Brain endothelium-targeted AAV1-X1 packaging\\neither control mCherry or Cre recombinase was systemically administered to Lrp6 Cre-conditional knockout\\nmice.\\nAfter three weeks, either AAV9-based PHP.eB or AAV9-X1.1 packaging eGFP was systemically delivered.\\nWhereas PHP.eB showed characteristic strong brain transduction regardless of AAV1-X1 cargo, AAV9-X1.1\\nbrain endothelial tropism was markedly reduced in the AAV1-X1-dosed mice with Lrp6 knocked out in AAV1-X1\\nIn Lrp6 knockout conditions, decreased AAV9-X1.1 liver transduction was also observed (Figure\\n4b and c).\\nTo confirm that LRP6 interaction is mediating the brain potency of AAV9-X1.1 in primates, we tested the\\nvector on macaque and human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (PBMECs) in culture (Figure 4d\\nand 4e).\\nof cell microarray screening to identify receptors for natural and engineered AAVs. We identify LRP6 as a novel\\nand highly conserved target for blood-brain barrier transcytosis by AAV9-X1.1, a potent engineered capsid for\\nprimate neurons 20 and human IL3 as an interaction partner for AAV9. These findings offer the prospect of\\nPrimate brain-enhanced AAVs gain interaction with LRP6 . a , Arraying AAV capsid-specific hits by\\nhuman brain endothelial cell expression levels reveals highly-conserved LRP6 as a potential receptor for BBB\\ncrossing. b , SPR confirms that the engineered capsids AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac gained direct binding\\ninteractions with human LRP6.\\nb, Representative sagittal brain images (left) and liver images (right) from the conditional Lrp6\\nknockout experiment. Imaging parameters were optimized independently for AAV9-X1.1 and AAV9-PHP.eB\\nsecond dose conditions. c , Quantification of AAV potency demonstrating that conditional knockout of Lrp6 in\\nand e, human primary brain microvascular endothelial cell culture, which decreases to AAV9 levels with Mesd\\ninhibition of LRP6. Bars indicate the mean value.\\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license .\\nIdentified cross-links were visualized\\nusing xiSPEC 79 .\\nPrimary cell culture potency assay\\nHuman brain microvascular endothelial cells (ScienCell Research Laboratories, cat# 1000) and cynomolgus\\nmonkey primary brain microvascular endothelial cells (CellBiologics, cat# MK-6023) were cultured as per the\\ninstructions provided by the vendor.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10250345\",\n      \"title\": \"Functional gene delivery to and across brain vasculature of ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Functional gene delivery to and across brain vasculature of ...\\nby X Chen · 2023 · Cited by 119 — endothelial-specific mouse capsids can be used to genetically engineer the blood-brain barrier. AAV-X1 and AAV-X1.1, which target brain endothelial cells\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.03.15.711945v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] LRP6-Guided Engineering of AAV9 Variants with Enhanced Blood\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"These authors contributed equally.\\novercome this limitation, we engineered a library of AAV9 capsid variants through rational\\ndesign focused on the low-density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 6 (LRP6), a\\nconserved mediator of transcytosis. A multi-tiered screening strategy, encompassing\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nhuman BBB endothelial cells followed by neuronal and glial target cells in vitro, identified\\nthree lead variants (QL9-21, QL9-22, and QL9-25) with markedly enhanced transduction\\npotential. In mice, these variants achieved a 5-28 fold increase in brain-wide gene delivery\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\ntypically mice, to enrich variants with enhanced brain transduction. This paradigm has\\nyielded breakthrough capsids like AAV-PHP.B, AAV-PHP.eB, AAV9-X1 and\\nAAV.CAP-Mac, demonstrating orders-of-magnitude improvement in CNS delivery in\\nrodents.\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nvariants for efficient and specific CNS gene delivery.\\nRESULTS\\nEngineered Variants Demonstrate Significantly Enhanced Transduction of Human\\nBBB Endothelial Cells in Vitro\\nTo identify AAV9 variants with enhanced BBB penetration potential, we first screened our\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nrationally designed, LRP6-targeted library (~100 variants) in vitro using the human\\ncerebral microvascular endothelial cell line hCMEC/D3, a representative model of the\\nBBB endothelium. Cells were infected with GFP-expressing AAV variants at varying\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\ngenome delivery across various brain regions and a concurrent 2.6 fold reduction in liver\\naccumulation compared to the parental AAV9 in cynomolgus macaques. These findings\\nCC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license .\\navailable under a\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nto mimic the in vivo journey of a systemically administered AAV vector: crossing the BBB\\nendothelium and subsequently infecting target neural cells. Initial in vitro screening using\\nhuman BBB endothelial cells (hCMEC/D3) enriched for variants with enhanced\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\n BBB Endothelial Cell Transduction: Human cerebral microvascular endothelial cells\\n(hCMEC/D3, ATCC) were seeded in 24- well plates at 1×10⁵ cells/well in ECM\\ncomplete medium (Gibco, A31961-01). The next day, cells at 60-80% confluence were\\nThese authors contributed equally.\\nLRP6 as blood-brain barrier transcytosis receptor and immune cytokine IL3 as AAV9 binder. Nat. Commun.\\n15, 7207. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0\\n14. Shay, T.F., Sullivan, E.E., Ding, X., Chen, X., Ravindra Kumar, S., Goertsen, D., Brown, D., Crosby, A.,\\nVielmetter, J., Borsos, M., et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://escholarship.org/content/qt2np080k1/qt2np080k1.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"https://escholarship.org/content/qt2np080k1/qt2np080k1.pdf\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"4,8 , Jonathan T. Ting 3,9 , Andrew S. Fox\\n6 ,\\nCagla Eroglu\\n2 & Viviana Gradinaru\\n1\\nDelivering genes to and across the brain vasculature ef fi ciently and speci fi cally\\nacross species remains a critical challenge for addressing neurological dis-\\neases. We have evolved adeno-associated virus (AAV9) capsids into vectors\\nthat transduce brain endothelial cells speci fi cally and ef fi ciently following\\nsystemic administration in wild-type mice with diverse genetic backgrounds,\\nand in rats. These AAVs also exhibit superior transduction of the CNS across\\nnon-human primates (marmosets and rhesus macaques), and in ex vivo human\\nbrain slices, although the endothelial tropism is not conserved across species.\\nThe capsid modi fi cations translate from AAV9 to other serotypes such as AAV1\\nand AAV-DJ, enabling serotype switching for sequential AAV administration in\\nmice. We demonstrate that the endothelial-speci fi c mouse capsids can be used\\nto genetically engineer the blood-brain barrier by transforming the mouse\\nbrain vasculature into a functional biofactory. We apply this approach to Hevin\\nknockout mice, where AAV-X1-mediated ectopic expression of the synapto-\\ngenic protein Sparcl1/Hevin in brain endothelial cells rescued synaptic de fi cits.\\nthalamocortical synaptic loss phenotype of Sparcl1/Hevin knockout\\nmice. We also demonstrate the transferability of AAV-X1 ’ s properties\\nfrom the AAV9 serotype to AAV1, enabling repeated AAV administra-\\ntion to increase CNS transduction. In summary, we provide novel\\nengineered systemic AAVs for brain endothelial-speci fi c cell targeting\\nviral vector for Hevin expression using our capsid AAV-X1.1. Indeed,\\nAAV-X1.1 packaging Hevin ef fi ciently transduced brain endothelial cells\\nand drove the expression of Hevin in these cells. To investigate whe-\\nther Hevin production via endothelial cells would rescue the de fi cits in\\nthalamocortical synapses observed in Hevin-KO mice, we retro-\\nwith a healthy BBB, X1 vectors seem to prefer endocytosis to trans-\\ncytosis at the BBB, either due to their interactions with novel receptors\\nor the vector ’ s own physiological features. In pericyte-de fi cient mice\\nwhere endothelial transcytosis is increased and endothelial cells show\\nperforming the experiments unless mentioned otherwise.\\nFig. 5 | Engineered AAVs can ef fi ciently transduce cultured human brain\\nmicrovascular endothelial cells and ex vivo macaque and human brain slices.\\nA (Left) Representative images of AAV (AAV2, AAV9, AAV-DJ, PHP.eB, PHP.V1, BR1,\\nX1, X1.1, X1.2, X1.3, X1.4, X1.5)-mediated eGFP expression (green) in human brain\\nmicrovascular endothelial cells (HBMECs). (AAVs packaged with ssAAV:CAG-eGFP,\\nn = 6 per condition, 1-day expression). (Right) Percentage of cells transduced by the\\nAAVs. In the condition of MOI:3E4, one-way ANOVA nonparametric Kruskal – Wallis\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-023-38582-7\",\n      \"title\": \"Functional gene delivery to and across brain vasculature of ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Functional gene delivery to and across brain vasculature of ...\\nby X Chen · 2023 · Cited by 119 — AAV-X1 and AAV-X1.1, which target brain endothelial cells specifically and efficiently following systemic delivery in mice with a ubiquitous\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea4995236ffe359cT2X0N360aM\"\n}"
