    "reasoning_content": "Good. p3:c5 supported: Lrp6 cKO mice, macaque and human PBMECs, hPSC neurons. The recorded quote covers mice + human BBB + hPSC neurons; macaque PBMECs supported elsewhere in the page. Since page is the nature article, SUPPORTED.\n\np1:c2: quote is verbatim in cell.com page per search snippet (\"We observed direct capsid-receptor interaction in a cell-free assay using surface plasmon resonance (SPR) analysis with recombinant ALPL, showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (K D) of ∼20 nm at pH 7.4 (Figure 4 C).\"). The item claims \"measured by SPR at the two pH values of the transcytosis pathway, one pair at a time, rather than by simulation.\" Page: pH-dependent dissociation measured by SPR at pH 7.4 and 5.5 (Fig 4D). Supported. But the quote itself only mentions pH 7.4. Still, the page states pH 5.5 dissociation by SPR. Also \"one pair at a time\" — SPR of capsid with ALPL. Fine → SUPPORTED.\n\nBut wait: the source URL cell.com returns 403 to me. Can I rely on search snippets? The instruction: \"Decide only from the pages you fetch\". I should attempt fetching cell.com via alternate mirror (biorxiv full text) which is the same paper preprint. The biorxiv version has the text. That's a fetched page. But it's a different URL than cited. Hmm. For the verdict, the question is whether the quote appears on the cited page and supports. I could try fetching cell.com abstract page with different path: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 maybe 403 too. Try doi.org.\n\nLet me try fetching the biorxiv full text PDF for p1:c2/c3 and the cell.com alternative.\n\np1:c3: quote \"We established a capsid transcytosis model using Madin-Darby canine kidney cells (MDCK) in a Transwell migration assay.\" is in cell.com snippet, so appears. Supports \"Transcytosis versus retention is decided with a cell-barrier assay, not with molecular simulation.\" Partly - page describing transcytosis model. OK SUPPORTED.\n\np1:c1: item claims \"~4E7-variant AAV9 peptide-display library is passaged in cynomolgus macaques and reduced to a focused set ... by the Capsid Discovery team (Tyler Moyer, Mathieu Nonnenmacher, Todd Carter).\" Quote about 1,500 variants of interest. Need page check for library size ~4E7 and authors. Let me fetch the biorxiv/cell version. Also check whether the recorded quote appears. Search snippet didn't include. Let me fetch biorxiv full text.\n\np1:done: item claims \"the single published pH-resolved capsid-receptor kinetics pair ... plus an ALPL-dependent Transwell transcytosis readout, but has not published computed pH-resolved MD contact lifetimes\". The negative claim (\"has not published\") — hard to verify; typical handling: the positive parts supported; the \"not published\" is a claim about absence. Given instructions, for a \"done\" item, check factual claims against sources. The quote supports the pH 5.5 rapid dissociation. The ALPL Transwell in Fig 4/paper. But \"the single published pair\" and \"not published computed MD\" is unverifiable. Possibly PARTIAL. Hmm. Consider what these \"done\" items are — claims about whether the lab has already run the target computation. Absence claims can't be verified from one page. I'd mark SUPPORTED if the page shows the experiments described and no MD; but \"no MD published\" is an absence across literature. I'll consider PARTIAL for done items where the positive part is supported and the absence is not verifiable... But that would render all done items PARTIAL. Let me think about the intended grading. The card's done item: \"X has already run the wet-lab analogue ... but not published computed MD\". The source page is the paper. The paper indeed presents SPR pH kinetics and Transwell transcytosis, and no MD contact-lifetime computation. A reasonable check: SUPPORTED if the paper contains those experiments and not MD. I'd say SUPPORTED for p1:done, maybe with note that absence of MD is consistent with the page.\n\nNow p2. Fetch dynotx pages. Also the substack.\n\nLet's batch fetch: dyno yp2 release, dyno two-capsids release, substack."
