    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_a558d0a096ce04c8a83754014980dbd5\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40501988\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nHere, we solve cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively.\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nOur findings demonstrate that rationally modulating AAVR affinity can alter liver targeting and cellular tropism.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.rcsb.org/structure/9P4Q\",\n      \"title\": \"RCSB PDB - 9P4Q: Cryo-EM structure of AAV.CAP-B10 complexed to AAVR-PKD2\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"9P4Q | pdb_00009p4q\\nCryo-EM structure of AAV.CAP-B10 complexed to AAVR-PKD2\\n9P4Q | pdb_00009p4q\\nLiterature\\nDownload Primary Citation\\n* Download Mendeley\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids.\\n9P4Q | pdb_00009p4q\\nIn mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\n9P4Q | pdb_00009p4q\\nHere, we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with the adeno-associated virus receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22- and 2.20-Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirmed by measuring affinities.\\n9P4Q | pdb_00009p4q\\nWe showed that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1, at 3.09 Å, and AAV9-X1.1 without and with PKD2, at 2.51 and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41906393\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids - PubMed\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-03-28\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nAffiliations Expand\\nAffiliations\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nTyler J Brittain et al. Mol Ther . 2026 .\\nFree article\\nShow details\\nDisplay options\\nDisplay options\\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\\nMol Ther\\nActions\\nDeveloping effective vectors for gene therapy requires accurate on-target coverage while minimizing off-target transduction that can lead to adverse events. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solved cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirmed by measuring affinities.\\nWe showed that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1, at 3.09 Å, and AAV9-X1.1 without and with PKD2, at 2.51 and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo.\\nOverall, we provide a framework for using structural information to guide rational engineering of gene delivery vectors to achieve safe and effective delivery.\\nCopyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Inc.\\nPubMed Disclaimer\\n* Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids.\\nBrittain TJ, Jang S, Coughlin GM, Barcelona BH, Giriat I, Ristic F, Appling N, Chossis CP, Shay TF, Gradinaru V. Brittain TJ, et al. bioRxiv [Preprint]. 2025 Jun 3:2025.06.02.655683. doi: 10.1101/2025.06.02.655683. bioRxiv. 2025. Update in: Mol Ther.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12157541\",\n      \"title\": \"\\n            Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids - PMC\\n        \",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nTyler J Brittain\\nTyler J Brittain\\n1 Division of Biology & Biological Engineering, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena CA 91125\\nFind articles by Tyler J Brittain\\n1, # , Seongmin Jang\\nSeongmin Jang\\nHere, we solve cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a structural motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirm by measuring affinities.\\nWe show that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1 and AAV9-X1.1, without and with PKD2, at 3.09, 2.51, and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo .\\nOverall, our findings demonstrate that rationally modulating AAVR affinity can alter liver targeting and cellular tropism.\\nAdditional engineering of PHP.eB at VR-IV by substitution of residues 452–458 with a randomized 7-mer identified a further improved version, AAV.CAP-B10 (CAP-B10) 23 , which more effectively de-targets from the liver in mice.\\nHowever, some of these modifications also make AAV transduction-deficient, stressing the importance of a trade-off between off-target reduction and on-target efficiency. Recent structural studies from our group and others showed that the moderately liver de-targeted PHP.eB has reduced affinity for PKD2 compared to AAV9 21 , 38 .\\nBased on this, we present seven cryo-EM structures of CNS-targeting and/or liver de-targeted AAVs, and their AAVR binding affinities. Based on the resulting structural insights, we rationally designed novel AAVs with limited liver transduction.\\nThe brain-enhanced capsids PHP.eB and CAP-B10 were sequentially derived from AAV9 along a directed evolution path ( Figure 1a - c , Table 1). This generated CAP-B10 from PHP.eB, which introduced neuronal cellular specificity and enhanced liver de-targeting.\\nTo investigate whether the VR-IV mutation in CAP-B10 impacts AAVR affinity, we determined high-resolution cryo-EM structures of three capsids: CAP-B10 (2.22 Å resolution), AAV9-X1 (3.09 Å) and AAV9-X1.1 (2.51 Å).\\nAAVR-dependent liver de-targeting is independent of brain sequestration\\nCAP-B10 is notable both for its liver de-targeting and for its enhanced specificity for neurons in the CNS 23 . To determine if both features arise from CAP-B10’s VR-IV 7-mer mediated loss of affinity for AAVR, we next tested whether an intermediate AAVR affinity would yield an intermediate vector tropism.\\nBy solving seven cryo-EM structures—five distinct AAVs and two AAV–AAVR-PKD2 complex structures—we reveal the molecular mechanism underlying this process. Our results reveal that CAP-B10 achieves improved liver de-targeting by reducing AAVR affinity through disrupting hydrogen bonds between the VR-IV loop and AAVR-PKD2.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://europepmc.org/article/PPR/PPR1030986\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-06-03\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nCrossing the blood-brain barrier while minimizing liver transduction is a key challenge in developing safe adeno-associated virus (AAV) vectors for treating brain disorders. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solve cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a structural motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirm by measuring affinities.\\nWe show that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1 and AAV9-X1.1, without and with PKD2, at 3.09, 2.51, and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo .\\nOverall, our findings demonstrate that rationally modulating AAVR affinity can alter liver targeting and cellular tropism.\\nLicense: CC BY-NC-ND.\\nSource: Europe PMC, EMBL-EBI.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1525001626002157\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nHere, we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with the adeno-associated virus receptor (AAVR) domain\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\ndi TJ Brittain · 2026 · Citato da 8 — We present seven cryo-EM structures of CNS targeting and/or liver de-targeted AAVs, along with their AAVR binding affinities. Based on the\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 6 — Tyler J. Brittain 1 3 we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures. AAVs achieve liver de-targeting, solving seven cryo-EM structures\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS ...\\nThe present work expands this understanding of the AAVR interface to VR-IV. Previously reported cryo-EM structures (PDB: 7WQO and 7WJX )\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism ...\\nby TJ Brittain · 2026 · Cited by 8 — AAV . CAP - B10 ( CAP - B10 ),24 which more effectively de-targets from the liver in mice. Furthermore, CAP - B10 shows a neuron-specific tropism with\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(26)00215-7\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids: Molecular Therapy\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Submit\\n* Log in\\n* ASGCT Member Log in\\n* Non-Member Log in\\n* Get Institutional Access\\n* Register\\n* open links dialog\\n  Access provided by\\n  Original Article Online now March 28, 2026 Open access\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nDeveloping effective vectors for gene therapy requires accurate on-target coverage while minimizing off-target transduction that can lead to adverse events. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solved cryo-EM structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with AAV receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22 and 2.20 Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirmed by measuring affinities.\\nWe showed that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1, at 3.09 Å, and AAV9-X1.1 without and with PKD2, at 2.51 and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo .\\nOverall, we provide a framework for using structural information to guide rational engineering of gene delivery vectors to achieve safe and effective delivery.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/2qscm-gs990\",\n      \"title\": \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids | CaltechAUTHORS\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"CaltechAUTHORS\\nCommunities\\nMy dashboard\\nLog in\\nCaltechAUTHORS\\nPublished July 1, 2026 | Version Published\\nJournal Article Open\\nStructural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\\nCreators\\nDeveloping effective vectors for gene therapy requires accurate on-target coverage while minimizing off-target transduction that can lead to adverse events. In mice, the engineered capsid PHP.eB shows enhanced brain transduction, while the further engineered CAP-B10 is also de-targeted from astrocytes and liver.\\nHere, we solved cryoelectron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of CAP-B10 and its complex with the adeno-associated virus receptor (AAVR) domain PKD2, at 2.22- and 2.20-Å resolutions, respectively. These structures reveal a motif that hinders AAVR binding, which we confirmed by measuring affinities.\\nWe showed that this motif is transferable to other capsids by solving cryo-EM structures of AAV9-X1, at 3.09 Å, and AAV9-X1.1 without and with PKD2, at 2.51 and 2.18 Å, respectively. Using this structural information, we designed and validated novel AAV variants with reduced liver and altered brain cell tropism in vivo .\\nperformed the confocal imaging. T.J.B. quantified liver de-targeting and brain tropism. S.J. quantified muscle transduction. G.M.C. and S.J. performed ddPCR. T.J.B. and S.J. wrote the manuscript and prepared the figures. V.G. supervised all aspects of the work.\\nCryo-EM maps and atomic models have been deposited with the accession codes EMDB (Electron Microscopy Data Bank): 71273 and PDB: 9P4L (CAP-B10),