Matthew Child](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Child%20M%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Rupinderjit Kaur](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Kaur%20R%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Emily Rouse](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Rouse%20E%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Kristin Graham](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Graham%20K%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Damien Maura](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Maura%20D%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Zachary Thorpe](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Thorpe%20Z%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Ambreen Sayed-Zahid](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Sayed-Zahid%20A%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Charlotte Hiu-Yan Chung](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Hiu-Yan%20Chung%20C%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Alexander Kutchin](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Kutchin%20A%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Amy Johnson](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Johnson%20A%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Johnny Yao](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Yao%20J%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Jeffrey Thompson](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Thompson%20J%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Nilesh Pande](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=%22Pande%20N%22\\\\[Author\\\\]) ¹ , [Mathieu E\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://scholars.mssm.edu/en/publications/highly-conserved-brain-vascular-receptor-alpl-mediates-transport-\",\n      \"title\": \"\\n        Highly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered AAV vectors across the blood-brain barrier\\n      -  Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-08-06\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Highly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered AAV vectors across the blood-brain barrier\\n* Tyler C. Moyer\\n* , Brett A. Hoffman\\n* , Weitong Chen\\n* , Ishan Shah\\n* , Xiao Qin Ren\\n* , Tatiana Knox\\n* , Jiachen Liu\\n* , Wei Wang\\n* , Jiangyu Li\\n* , Hamza Khalid\\n* , Anupriya S. Kulkarni\\n* , Munachiso Egbuchulam\\n* , Joseph Clement\\n* , Alexis Bloedel\\n* , Matthew Child\\n* , Rupinderjit Kaur\\n* , Emily Rouse\\n* , Kristin Graham\\n* , Damien Maura\\n* , Zachary Thorpe Ambreen Sayed-Zahid, Charlotte Hiu-Yan Chung, Alexander Kutchin, Amy Johnson, Johnny Yao, Jeffrey Thompson, Nilesh Pande, Mathieu E. Nonnenmacher Show 8 others Show less\\nResearch output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\\n32 Scopus citations\\n* Overview\\n* Fingerprint\\nHere, we apply our RNA-guided TRACER AAV capsid evolution platform to generate VCAP-102, an AAV9 variant with markedly increased brain tropism following intravenous delivery in both rodents and primates. Relative to AAV9, VCAP-102 demonstrates 20- to 400-fold increased gene transfer across multiple brain regions.\\nWe identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model.\\n* APA\\n* Author\\n* BIBTEX\\n* Harvard\\n* Standard\\n* RIS\\n* Vancouver\\nMoyer, T. C., Hoffman, B. A., Chen, W., Shah, I., Ren, X. Q., Knox, T., Liu, J., Wang, W., Li, J., Khalid, H., Kulkarni, A. S., Egbuchulam, M., Clement, J., Bloedel, A., Child, M., Kaur, R., Rouse, E., Graham, K., Maura, D., ... Nonnenmacher, M. E. (2025).\\nHighly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered AAV vectors across the blood-brain barrier . Molecular Therapy , 33 (8), 3902-3916. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymthe.2025.04.046\\nMoyer, Tyler C. ; Hoffman, Brett A. ; Chen, Weitong et al.\\n/ Highly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered AAV vectors across the blood-brain barrier . In: Molecular Therapy . 2025 ; Vol. 33, No. 8. pp. 3902-3916.\\n@article{df0155e74bbb47cd84fcc55beadac301,\\nauthor = \\\"Moyer, {Tyler C.} and Hoffman, {Brett A.} and Weitong Chen and Ishan Shah and Ren, {Xiao Qin} and Tatiana Knox and Jiachen Liu and Wei Wang and Jiangyu Li and Hamza Khalid and Kulkarni, {Anupriya S.} and Munachiso Egbuchulam and Joseph Clement and Alexis Bloedel and Matthew Child and Rupinderjit Kaur and Emily Rouse and Kristin Graham\\nand Damien Maura and Zachary Thorpe and Ambreen Sayed-Zahid and {Hiu-Yan Chung}, Charlotte and Alexander Kutchin and Amy Johnson and Johnny Yao and Jeffrey Thompson and Nilesh Pande and Nonnenmacher, {Mathieu E.}\\\",\\nnote = \\\"Publisher Copyright: {\\\\textcopyright} 2025 The Author(s)\\\",\\nyear = \\\"2025\\\",\\nmonth = aug,\\nday = \\\"6\\\",\\ndoi = \\\"10.1016/j.ymthe.2025.04.046\\\",\\nlanguage = \\\"English\\\",\\nvolume = \\\"33\\\",\\npages = \\\"3902--3916\\\",\\njournal = \\\"Molecular Therapy\\\",\\nissn = \\\"1525-0016\\\",\\npublisher = \\\"Cell Press\\\",\\nnumber = \\\"8\\\",\\n}\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1.full.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"1 Highly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"1\\nHighly conserved brain vascular receptor ALPL mediates transport of engineered viral vectors across\\nthe blood-brain barrier\\nAuthors:\\nTyler C. Moyer 1‡ , Brett A. Hoffman 1‡ , Weitong Chen 1 , Ishan Shah 1 , Xiao-Qin Ren 1 , Tatiana Knox 1 , Jiachen\\nLiu 1 , Wei Wang 1 , Jiangyu Li 1 , Hamza Khalid 1 , Anupriya S.\\nNonnenmacher 1*\\n1 Voyager Therapeutics, 75 Hayden Ave, Lexington MA 02421, USA\\n‡ These authors contributed equally to this work\\n* Corresponding author: mnonnenmacher@vygr.com\\nAbstract:\\nDelivery of systemically administered therapeutics to the central nervous system (CNS) is restricted by the\\nblood-brain barrier (BBB). Bioengineered Adeno-Associated Virus (AAV) capsids have been shown to\\ndemonstrate that the enhanced CNS tropism of VCAP-102 results from direct interaction with alkaline\\nphosphatase (ALPL), a highly conserved membrane-associated protein expressed on the brain vasculature.\\nVCAP-102 interacts with human, primate and murine ALPL isoforms, and ectopic expression of ALPL is\\nsufficient to initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis of VCAP-102 in an in vitro transwell model. Our work\\nidentifies VCAP-102 as a cross-species CNS gene delivery vector with a strong potential for clinical\\ntranslation and establishes ALPL as a brain delivery shuttle capable of efficient BBB transport to maximize\\nCNS delivery of biotherapeutics.\\nThe three vascular receptors identified so far are LY6A 18,19 , LY6C1 20,21 and CA4 21 ,\\nused by AAV-PHP.B, 9P39 and 9P36 10 capsids, respectively. All three receptors belong to the\\nglycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored protein family and show a very low degree of conservation\\nacross species.\\ntogether with reduced liver delivery in all animal models. We identify the Tissue-Nonspecific Alkaline\\nPhosphatase isozyme (TNAP or ALPL, OMIM 171760), a GPI-anchored protein encoded by the ALPL\\ngene, as the vascular receptor utilized by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB. We show that ectopic expression of\\nALPL in a polarized cell layer is sufficient to mediate transcytosis of VCAP-102, and provide in vivo\\nevidence that capsid-receptor interaction is directly correlated with brain transduction. Collectively, our\\nwork identifies a cross-species, neurotrophic viral vector with strong translational potential, and identifies\\nALPL, a highly conserved membrane-anchored receptor, as an efficient shuttle for transporting therapeutics\\nacross the BBB.\\nResults:\\nDiscovery of a cross-species BBB-penetrant AAV capsid\\nTowards creating an AAV peptide display library with optimal structural and functional properties, we\\nTNAP) encoded by the ALPL gene, as the most specific interaction partner. ALPL is a highly conserved\\nGPI-anchored cell surface protein with closely related orthologs across mammals, and is expressed in\\nmultiple tissues including liver, bone, kidney and brain 25 . In the brain, ALPL expression is mainly detected\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea4995236ffe359cT2X0N360aM\"\n}"
